## 📝 Writing E2E Tests

### Test Structure

```typescript
import { test, expect } from './helpers/extensionContext';

test.describe('Feature Name', () => {
  test('should do specific thing', async ({ context, extensionId }) => {
    // Arrange: Setup test data and state
    const popup = await context.newPage();
    await popup.goto(`chrome-extension://${extensionId}/popup.html`);
    
    // Act: Perform the action
    await popup.click('[data-testid="some-button"]');
    
    // Assert: Verify the result
    await expect(popup.locator('[data-testid="result"]')).toBeVisible();
  });
});
```

### Test Naming Convention

- ✅ `should show error when email is invalid`
- ✅ `should save credential after successful login`
- ✅ `should not autofill on HTTP sites`
- ❌ `test login` (too vague)
- ❌ `checkEmailValidation` (not descriptive)

### Always Include Console Monitoring

```typescript
test('feature name', async ({ context, extensionId }) => {
  const popup = await context.newPage();
  
  // Monitor console
  const logs: string[] = [];
  const errors: string[] = [];
  
  popup.on('console', msg => {
    logs.push(`[${msg.type()}] ${msg.text()}`);
    if (msg.type() === 'error') errors.push(msg.text());
  });
  
  // Your test code...
  
  // Print logs for debugging
  console.log('Console logs:', logs);
  
  // Verify no unexpected errors
  const unexpectedErrors = errors.filter(e => !e.includes('DevTools'));
  expect(unexpectedErrors).toHaveLength(0);
});
```

